{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Architectural analysis: the fsck cluster's concern map\n\nOperator-ordered framing: not a mechanical split. Below is the concern map, the recurrence\nmechanism that keeps pinning this file at the cap, and why the cuts land where they do.\n\n## The cluster today\n\n| module | LOC | concerns it holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_commands/fsck.py` | **800** | FOUR fused: per-ticket store walk, tracker-level health, JSON report shaping, CLI/argv + repair hand-off |\n| `_commands/fsck_repair.py` | 625 | the mutating `--repair` surface, plus the two filesystem primitives (`_ticket_dirs`, `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`) diagnostics also need |\n| `_commands/fsck_authorship.py` | 140 | one concern: the per-env authorship tally (check 4.8) |\n| `_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py` | 684 | a *different command* (bridge audits: `audit_store_integrity` / `audit_bridge_mappings` / `audit_binding_drift` + its own `main`/`_format_report`). Shares the word \"fsck\" and nothing else — no call edge to `_commands/fsck.py`. Deliberately out of scope. |\n\n## Why this file keeps hitting the cap (the recurrence)\n\n`fsck.py` is the *aggregation point* for checks. Checks 4.5, 4.6, 4.7, 4.8, 4.9 and 5 were each\nadded inline to `_scan` or its immediate neighbours, so the module grows monotonically with the\nnumber of checks while its four concerns stay fused. Two prior remediations already show both\nhalves of the answer: `fsck_repair` pulled the *mutating* concern out (one-way, diagnostic ->\nrepair), and `fsck_authorship` pulled *one check with its own state* out. The remaining growth\npressure is the diagnostic side, which was never separated by concern.\n\nThe seam was in fact already cut once and not followed through: `_tracker_health` exists purely\nto stop `_scan` growing for every tracker-level check added — an in-file grouping of 4.5/4.6/\n4.7/4.9 with a single call site. That grouping is the natural module boundary.\n\n## The concerns, and where I cut\n\n1. **Enumeration + per-ticket validators** -> new `fsck_scan.py`.\n   `_scan` (the store walk: JSON validity, CREATE presence, index.lock, snapshot consistency,\n   forward-compat types, and the advisory ensures/authorship tallies) and `_check_snapshot`\n   (the per-snapshot validator it calls). Existing seam: `_check_snapshot` is called from\n   exactly one place, `_scan`'s inner `_snap_findings`; nothing else in the module calls into\n   either. This is the cluster that grows per check.\n2. **Tracker-level health** -> new `fsck_tracker_health.py`.\n   `_tracker_health` + the four checks it fans out to (`_tracker_sync_status`,\n   `_branch_mismatch`, `_foreign_store_paths`, `foreign_store_path_list`) and the\n   `_FSCK_GIT_TIMEOUT` watchdog they all share. Existing seam: `_tracker_health` is already the\n   single entry point, called once from `_scan`; the four helpers are called from nowhere else\n   in the module. These share a shape the per-ticket checks do not — each inspects the tracker\n   as a whole via git/config, each yields at most one line, each decides for itself whether the\n   line is counted. That shape is the concern.\n3. **Report shaping + command driver** -> stays in `fsck.py`.\n   `_transform_json`/`_STRUCTURED_KINDS` (the text->JSON derivation) plus `fsck_cli`,\n   `_repair_cli`, `_run_live_repair`, `_missing_tracker_result`. Report shaping is only ~45 LOC\n   — extracting it would create a sub-100-LOC module, which the policy forbids, and it is the\n   output half of the same command surface the driver owns. So `fsck.py` becomes what its name\n   should mean: *the fsck command* — parse argv, drive scan or repair, render.\n\nResulting dependency direction is strictly one-way and matches the existing call graph:\n`fsck.py` (driver) -> `fsck_scan.py` -> {`fsck_tracker_health.py`, `fsck_repair.py`,\n`fsck_authorship.py`}. No cycles, no new edges.\n\n## What I deliberately did NOT do\n\n- No `fsck2.py`-style numeric split, and no cut that would produce a module under 100 LOC\n  (this is why report shaping stays with the driver).\n- `bridge_fsck.py` untouched: no call edge, different command, different concern.\n- `fsck_repair.py` untouched: it is already a clean single-concern extraction. Its ownership of\n  `_ticket_dirs`/`_resolve_tracker_git_dir` is a pre-existing wart (filesystem primitives\n  living in the *repair* module because it was extracted first) — noted, not fixed here, to\n  keep this behavior-preserving.\n\n## Compatibility constraint the cuts must respect\n\n`fsck.<symbol>` attribute access is load-bearing outside this module: `_store/gitutil.py` and\n`_commands/fsck_recover.py` import `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` from it, `tracker_maintenance.py`\nimports `foreign_store_path_list`, and tests call `fsck._check_snapshot` / `fsck._scan` /\n`fsck._tracker_sync_status` / `fsck._foreign_store_paths`. All of these *call* the symbols; none\nmonkeypatch them, so module-level re-exports preserve them. The two symbols that ARE\nmonkeypatched on the `fsck` module (`_reconciler_in_flight`, `owned_repair_pause`) are consumed\nby `_run_live_repair`, which stays in `fsck.py` — so the flamingo trap (a moved function\nresolving a patched dependency from the wrong module's globals) does not arise.\n\n## Pattern for future near-cap remediations\n\nCut by *concern shape*, not by line count: find the check/helper family that already has a\nsingle call site (that call site is the seam the previous author cut and stopped at), give it a\nmodule named for what it inspects, and keep the entry-point module as the command surface. If a\ncandidate concern is under 100 LOC, it belongs with its nearest collaborator, not in its own file."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786599220869269001,"uuid":"9251bad4-b4ad-40dc-91e6-515929b0379d"}